1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,720
In July 1969, the rocket that carried
the crew of the first Moon landing

2
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:08,700
weighed about two thousand
nine hundred tonnes on the launch pad.

3
00:00:09,269 --> 00:00:13,689
Eight days later, what came back to Earth
was a capsule with three people inside.

4
00:00:14,409 --> 00:00:16,269
It weighed just under five tonnes.

5
00:00:17,069 --> 00:00:20,549
About one part in six hundred
of that rocket came home.

6
00:00:21,129 --> 00:00:22,709
So where did the rest go?

7
00:00:23,996 --> 00:00:28,076
Most of it was propellant:
fuel, and the oxygen to burn it.

8
00:00:28,716 --> 00:00:31,996
More than two thousand tonnes of it
burnt in under three minutes.

9
00:00:32,534 --> 00:00:34,394
And here's the strange part.

10
00:00:35,034 --> 00:00:37,274
Much of it wasn't there
to lift the spacecraft.

11
00:00:38,014 --> 00:00:40,154
It was there to lift more propellant.

12
00:00:40,734 --> 00:00:44,254
To keep things simple,
we'll call all of it fuel from here on.

13
00:00:46,774 --> 00:00:49,134
Imagine you want a rocket to go faster.

14
00:00:49,814 --> 00:00:51,814
The obvious answer is more fuel.

15
00:00:52,325 --> 00:00:53,825
But fuel has mass.

16
00:00:54,305 --> 00:00:56,825
Before the rocket can burn it,
it has to carry it,

17
00:00:57,055 --> 00:00:59,305
and speed it up along
with everything else.

18
00:00:59,754 --> 00:01:02,494
So the extra fuel needs fuel of its own.

19
00:01:02,754 --> 00:01:04,994
And that fuel needs fuel too.

20
00:01:05,612 --> 00:01:08,132
At some point, adding fuel
stops solving the problem...

21
00:01:08,362 --> 00:01:10,652
and starts becoming the problem.

22
00:01:13,222 --> 00:01:17,062
This problem has an equation,
and it's more than a century old.

23
00:01:17,734 --> 00:01:21,649
In 1903, a schoolteacher
from the Russian town of Kaluga,

24
00:01:21,879 --> 00:01:24,474
Konstantin Tsiolkovsky,
published the formula

25
00:01:24,704 --> 00:01:27,074
that every rocket still has to obey.

26
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:29,590
He wasn't the first to do the maths.

27
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:34,410
In 1813, a British mathematician,
William Moore, had worked out

28
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:37,670
how a rocket speeds up as it loses mass,
for military rockets.

29
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:42,350
Tsiolkovsky used it to work
out how we might reach space.

30
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,060
Here it is.

31
00:01:46,613 --> 00:01:50,813
On the left is delta-v: the change
in speed the rocket can achieve.

32
00:01:51,615 --> 00:01:53,895
On the right, first, the exhaust speed.

33
00:01:54,815 --> 00:01:57,075
A rocket has nothing to push against.

34
00:01:57,815 --> 00:02:01,435
It moves by throwing mass backwards,
and the faster it throws it,

35
00:02:01,515 --> 00:02:06,155
the more momentum each kilogram carries
off, and the more the rocket gets back.

36
00:02:06,577 --> 00:02:10,217
Then the ratio of two masses:
the rocket full of fuel at the start,

37
00:02:10,447 --> 00:02:12,637
and what's left when the fuel is gone.

38
00:02:13,317 --> 00:02:15,577
Engines, empty tanks, structure...

39
00:02:15,897 --> 00:02:18,257
and the payload
you actually wanted to send.

40
00:02:18,741 --> 00:02:21,521
And wrapped around that ratio,
a logarithm.

41
00:02:21,781 --> 00:02:24,241
That's the part that makes it so hard.

42
00:02:25,761 --> 00:02:28,101
Take two rockets with the same engine.

43
00:02:28,841 --> 00:02:32,601
The first starts four times heavier
than it will be with its tanks empty.

44
00:02:33,341 --> 00:02:35,441
The second, eight times heavier.

45
00:02:36,059 --> 00:02:37,659
Twice the mass ratio.

46
00:02:38,039 --> 00:02:39,779
You'd expect twice the speed.

47
00:02:40,379 --> 00:02:42,439
It gets about fifty percent more.

48
00:02:43,492 --> 00:02:48,012
Double it again, from eight to sixteen,
and you get only about a third more.

49
00:02:48,592 --> 00:02:52,692
Every extra tonne of fuel buys less speed
than the tonne before it.

50
00:02:53,211 --> 00:02:55,371
Turn that around, and it looks worse.

51
00:02:56,451 --> 00:03:00,331
Each extra kilometre per second
doesn't add to the rocket's starting mass.

52
00:03:00,831 --> 00:03:02,351
It multiplies it.

53
00:03:04,801 --> 00:03:07,021
So how much speed does orbit take?

54
00:03:07,821 --> 00:03:10,121
In low Earth orbit, a spacecraft travels

55
00:03:10,351 --> 00:03:13,621
at about seven point eight kilometres
per second, sideways.

56
00:03:14,499 --> 00:03:18,019
On the way up, the rocket also fights
gravity and the air.

57
00:03:18,519 --> 00:03:22,679
Altogether, it needs around
nine point four kilometres per second.

58
00:03:23,323 --> 00:03:27,623
Now give it the best chemical engine,
burning hydrogen and oxygen.

59
00:03:28,523 --> 00:03:32,343
And let's be generous: assume
it performs as it would in a vacuum,

60
00:03:32,423 --> 00:03:35,543
with exhaust at four and a half
kilometres per second.

61
00:03:36,178 --> 00:03:39,558
Put those numbers into the equation,
and the rocket has to start

62
00:03:39,788 --> 00:03:42,078
about eight times heavier than it ends.

63
00:03:42,802 --> 00:03:45,062
A hundred tonnes on the launch pad...

64
00:03:45,142 --> 00:03:48,102
and only about twelve tonnes
allowed to reach orbit.

65
00:03:48,629 --> 00:03:50,569
And those twelve tonnes aren't payload.

66
00:03:51,409 --> 00:03:55,509
They're the engines, the empty tanks,
the whole structure that held the fuel.

67
00:03:56,089 --> 00:03:58,469
Whatever's left after that
is the satellite.

68
00:03:59,267 --> 00:04:01,887
In real rockets, that leftover is small.

69
00:04:02,687 --> 00:04:06,367
A Falcon 9 weighs about
five hundred and fifty tonnes at launch.

70
00:04:07,247 --> 00:04:10,807
Its maximum payload to low orbit
is around twenty-three tonnes.

71
00:04:11,407 --> 00:04:13,267
Up to about four percent.

72
00:04:14,369 --> 00:04:19,149
So rockets that reach orbit shed
stages or other hardware on the way up.

73
00:04:19,671 --> 00:04:22,351
That's where the rest
of the Saturn V went.

74
00:04:23,431 --> 00:04:27,991
Its first stage burned for less than three
minutes, then fell away into the Atlantic.

75
00:04:28,711 --> 00:04:31,491
Five engines and enormous empty tanks...

76
00:04:31,651 --> 00:04:34,391
that the rest of the flight
no longer had to carry.

77
00:04:34,985 --> 00:04:38,485
The second stage took it almost to orbit,
and fell away too.

78
00:04:39,005 --> 00:04:43,545
The third finished the job, and later
pushed the spacecraft towards the Moon.

79
00:04:44,149 --> 00:04:48,129
Each stage does part of the work,
and then stops being dead weight.

80
00:04:48,949 --> 00:04:52,629
The stages above it never have to carry
its empty tanks and engines.

81
00:04:53,442 --> 00:04:55,102
That's why so many rockets look like

82
00:04:55,332 --> 00:04:57,762
several rockets stacked
on top of each other.

83
00:04:58,302 --> 00:04:59,502
They are.

84
00:05:00,097 --> 00:05:02,497
But staging doesn't beat
the rocket equation.

85
00:05:03,317 --> 00:05:05,517
Every stage obeys it on its own.

86
00:05:06,017 --> 00:05:09,837
It just stops us paying for the same
empty tank over and over again.

87
00:05:12,317 --> 00:05:14,477
Apollo kept doing it all the way.

88
00:05:14,877 --> 00:05:17,517
The lander left its lower
half on the Moon.

89
00:05:18,077 --> 00:05:22,377
Its upper half brought the astronauts
back up, and was cast off in lunar orbit.

90
00:05:23,105 --> 00:05:26,525
The service module was dropped
just before re-entry.

91
00:05:26,885 --> 00:05:29,785
What came home was the one piece
that had to.

92
00:05:31,965 --> 00:05:34,125
So why not just build a better engine?

93
00:05:35,045 --> 00:05:37,325
Remember the exhaust
speed in the equation.

94
00:05:38,125 --> 00:05:40,925
The faster the exhaust,
the less fuel you need.

95
00:05:41,537 --> 00:05:44,077
Chemical rockets can't go much faster.

96
00:05:44,757 --> 00:05:48,897
Their exhaust speed is limited by the
energy released in chemical reactions.

97
00:05:49,891 --> 00:05:53,151
Ion engines throw their propellant out
several times faster.

98
00:05:53,381 --> 00:05:56,971
NASA's Dawn probe used them
to change its own speed

99
00:05:57,201 --> 00:06:00,091
by more than eleven kilometres per second.

100
00:06:00,541 --> 00:06:02,141
But their push was tiny.

101
00:06:02,221 --> 00:06:05,821
About the weight of a sheet of paper
resting on your hand.

102
00:06:06,506 --> 00:06:10,826
At full power, Dawn would have needed
four days to reach sixty miles an hour.

103
00:06:11,606 --> 00:06:14,526
An engine like that
can't lift anything off the ground.

104
00:06:15,201 --> 00:06:17,481
To leave the ground, you need brute force.

105
00:06:18,301 --> 00:06:20,781
To travel efficiently,
you need fast exhaust.

106
00:06:21,601 --> 00:06:24,021
No engine we've ever flown
is good at both.

107
00:06:26,791 --> 00:06:28,671
Which leaves one more question.

108
00:06:29,391 --> 00:06:33,631
We've been treating that nine point four
kilometres per second as a fixed price.

109
00:06:33,711 --> 00:06:36,191
But the price is set by Earth.

110
00:06:36,875 --> 00:06:38,855
A heavier planet pulls harder.

111
00:06:39,415 --> 00:06:41,215
Escaping it takes more speed...

112
00:06:41,555 --> 00:06:44,395
and in the rocket equation,
more speed doesn't add to the bill.

113
00:06:44,475 --> 00:06:45,955
It multiplies it.

114
00:06:47,067 --> 00:06:50,087
In 2018,
the German researcher Michael Hippke

115
00:06:50,167 --> 00:06:54,347
did the calculation
for a rocky planet ten times Earth's mass,

116
00:06:54,587 --> 00:06:57,287
and about one point seven times as wide.

117
00:06:57,979 --> 00:07:02,259
To escape Earth, a rocket needs
about eleven kilometres per second.

118
00:07:03,019 --> 00:07:04,779
There, it would need about twenty-seven.

119
00:07:06,055 --> 00:07:08,615
In his simplified single-stage rocket,

120
00:07:08,895 --> 00:07:10,755
the mass ratio jumps from about twenty-six

121
00:07:10,835 --> 00:07:13,723
to about two thousand seven hundred.

122
00:07:14,023 --> 00:07:16,123
A mission like Apollo, he estimated,

123
00:07:16,583 --> 00:07:20,163
would need a rocket of around
four hundred thousand tonnes.

124
00:07:20,243 --> 00:07:22,823
More than a hundred Saturn Vs.

125
00:07:24,001 --> 00:07:26,281
His calculation suggests
that chemical rockets

126
00:07:26,511 --> 00:07:29,001
could still escape a planet like this.

127
00:07:29,821 --> 00:07:31,621
But on heavier rocky worlds,

128
00:07:31,721 --> 00:07:35,121
a chemical rocket's fuel needs
could become prohibitive.

129
00:07:36,083 --> 00:07:38,003
It works the other way, too.

130
00:07:38,503 --> 00:07:40,823
Mars has about a tenth of Earth's mass.

131
00:07:41,523 --> 00:07:45,583
To escape it, a rocket needs
only about five kilometres per second.

132
00:07:46,210 --> 00:07:48,370
In the same simplified calculation,

133
00:07:48,790 --> 00:07:52,290
the mass ratio drops from about twenty-six
to about four.

134
00:07:54,455 --> 00:07:57,075
The rocket equation
never forbids anything.

135
00:07:57,555 --> 00:07:59,135
It only names the price.

136
00:08:01,235 --> 00:08:04,575
On Earth, that price is a rocket
that throws away almost all of itself

137
00:08:04,805 --> 00:08:07,095
to bring one small capsule home.

138
00:08:07,755 --> 00:08:11,935
On a heavier world, it might be a price
nobody would ever choose to pay.

139
00:08:13,725 --> 00:08:15,625
So here's a question for you.

140
00:08:16,085 --> 00:08:20,005
Do you think intelligent life on a much
heavier planet could ever reach space?

141
00:08:20,505 --> 00:08:22,075
Let me know in the comments.
